WEBVTT

NOTE automatic transcription, uncorrected

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
What questions should one ask about Cedric today?

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.000
There are so many.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.000
But I'll start just with one.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.000
It's the mode of thinking.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.000
What architecture does.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:24.000
And architecture is also about questioning the society in which we live.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:33.700
And it is also about providing, inventing questions and providing potential answers.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:37.500
Cedric did it in an extraordinary manner.

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:41.680
And I'm still, 50 years later, I'm still interested in this.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:47.414
At the same time, something else has happened today, which is not necessarily for

00:00:47.414 --> 00:00:48.060
the better,

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:56.723
which is the over-reliance to simply hyper-realist images, which go exactly against

00:00:56.723 --> 00:00:58.080
the thinking.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:07.896
And so this question of finding a mode of notation that allows you to develop an

00:01:07.896 --> 00:01:09.000
argument,

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:18.517
And as opposed to finding simply hyper-realist renderings that provide the supposed

00:01:18.517 --> 00:01:19.320
answers

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:26.244
to questions that were never asked, I think that's perhaps one of the questions that

00:01:26.244 --> 00:01:27.480
is being raised

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:36.415
by this discussion that we have about Cedric these days. As you know, I was always

00:01:36.415 --> 00:01:36.960
quite

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:43.500
interested in what Cedric was doing already at the time and yes indeed it has

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:54.080
had an influence and probably still now in some sort of a way. Namely that

00:01:54.080 --> 00:02:01.200
the architect is there, yes, to ask questions. Not necessarily to provide any

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:07.560
any answer that can satisfy the market or a particular client.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.980
It's really looking at society as it is

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:16.320
and seeing opportunities for invention.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.240
I'll take two projects that we all know,

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:22.380
the Fun Palace or the Potteries Thinkbelt.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:26.720
In themselves, they are not a burning need

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:28.380
for the society at the time.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:30.640
Nobody was thinking about it.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.480
but it was there.

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:36.600
And the potential that was raised,

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.160
thanks to asking these questions,

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:41.580
was enormous and had an influence

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:46.580
that did reverberate for 20, 30 years after that.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:52.120
I found a profoundly, let's say,

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:57.400
a introspective person

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:03.240
that was simultaneously, constantly walking on a tight rope.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:10.380
In other words, when he would give lectures, now in public,

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:16.040
the mode of lecturing, which if you've ever tried,

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:21.520
of using individual cards which have statements

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:24.920
and shuffling the cards in any possible order,

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:33.680
and then trying to develop an argument out of the random appearance of each of

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:40.860
those questions or comments is not an easy thing to do. It requires an agility

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:46.600
of the mind which he possessed to a great degree. So when he was working

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:54.420
around certain theme that I was familiar with it was certainly an easy you know

00:03:54.420 --> 00:04:01.260
dialogue but when he was taking other courses of conversation such as cooking

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:04.920
or things of this sort then he would get me completely lost.

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:13.480
Cédric's most significant project, I mean I would say the project of his life, i.e.

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:25.840
trying to construct architecture as a form of philosophical endeavor, as a

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:34.040
form of research, and I would use in my own words as a form of knowledge and not

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:40.240
simply as knowledge of form. After that we can go and look at a series of

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:45.400
of individual projects and we can depending on the context in which we

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:52.180
we operate we can say this this or that but I think the larger issue is rather

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:58.840
considering that architecture can be something more than simply the answer to

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:05.320
one specific you know question well you know the history of architecture is

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:11.560
filled with people who had a lot of influence and built little. Take

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:17.620
Piranesi for example, a major, major influence. Take Boulet, Ledoux and Lequeux,

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:24.940
three of the so-called utopian socialists in France, enormous influence.

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:37.700
Take in the at the time of same periods as Cedric was active, people like

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:44.320
yeah I mean take Virilio and Paran with the oblique function that's later on

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:51.880
Jona Friedman for example. So you have many many people who had an influence

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:57.740
beyond the act of building, and I would say that even a lesson to learn about

00:05:57.740 --> 00:06:02.240
what architecture is. Architecture is a mode of thinking and not only a mode of

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:05.640
building. And I think this is really important, and there are some cultures

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:10.400
and some natures that refuse the idea that architecture is a mode of thinking.

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:16.680
I remember one famous Pritzker Prize architect saying that at the end all the

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:23.520
writing disappears only the building remains. I would might I might want to

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:29.100
argue the opposite. Even though I think both of them the building and the

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:34.380
writing tend to reinforce one another and that's probably what makes it the

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:39.180
most interesting. I think if the opportunity had a reason for him to

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:45.500
build more than he has I think he would have seized upon the opportunity. The

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:55.320
reason is that building actually helps you phrase further questions, right? In my

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:59.940
particular case I've always found fascinating the back and forth between

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:05.120
not only theory and practice but between certain way to raise questions, certain

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:12.500
way to answers which are not the only ones and then that bouncing from

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:21.365
the real to the virtual, from the real to the totally, totally theoretical, is an

00:07:21.365 --> 00:07:22.460
incredibly

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:26.680
powerful mode of doing things.

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:35.292
So I think that it would have been good for Cedric to find himself in the situation

00:07:35.292 --> 00:07:35.500
of

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:42.460
having the shoes in the mud and raising other questions.

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:45.620
I won the competition of the Parc de la Villette.

00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:48.680
And at the time, at the beginning of the competition,

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:50.320
I won it as a master plan.

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:54.960
In other words, I was to organize not only

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:57.300
some of my own building, but have also

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:00.780
other buildings of people who would join us.

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:04.640
So I started to invite some of my friends.

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:07.640
And there was John Haydack.

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:11.080
There was Peter Eisenman that I paired with Jack Derrida.

00:08:11.080 --> 00:08:14.480
And there was, of course, Cedric.

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:18.360
And Cedric was very excited about the idea of doing it.

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:22.880
He was very, actually very supportive of my project.

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:25.100
So everything seems to go well.

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:30.100
However, it was also a time when money was getting short.

00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:34.260
And the government was changing.

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:37.220
It was an extremely complicated affair.

00:08:37.220 --> 00:08:40.280
And at the time, it had been said

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:43.280
that it should be something which is now familiar

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:47.600
to our ears, a PPP, a public-private partnership

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:52.600
between the people who would sponsor the building

00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:59.600
that I had asked Cedric to work on.

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:02.840
What I had asked Cedric to work on was a greenhouse.

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:08.340
And that greenhouse had to be financed simultaneously

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:15.300
by a large horticultural, not quite multinational corporation,

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:21.220
but a large operator selling seeds and trees and things

00:09:21.220 --> 00:09:22.560
of this sort.

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:27.400
So Cedric designed, actually, if I remember correctly,

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:32.780
two schemes for a site that I had given him.

00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:36.020
The principle was they were the greater fallas,

00:09:36.020 --> 00:09:40.120
and then you would attach to each of the Follets

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:43.920
one of those buildings done by another architect.

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:47.320
So in the case of Cedric's greenhouse,

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:49.900
it was actually quite beautiful, very straightforward.

00:09:49.900 --> 00:09:54.660
It was a half cylinder with activities happening

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:56.480
at different levels within it.

00:09:58.140 --> 00:10:03.000
In many ways, sometimes I say laughingly

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:07.140
that the zoo here in Paris,

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:10.400
I just finished the Paris Zoo a few months ago,

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:16.220
that the greenhouse has some bit of an homage

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:19.940
to Cedric in its simplicity.

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:25.000
Unfortunately, back to La Villette,

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:29.180
the arrangement between the person

00:10:29.180 --> 00:10:32.880
or the company that was supposed to finance Cedric project

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:38.000
and the park could never be finalized

00:10:38.840 --> 00:10:41.460
and the project never went beyond,

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:44.080
you know, call it design development.

00:10:44.080 --> 00:10:49.080
One thing which is very clear about the scene

00:10:49.580 --> 00:10:52.920
that anybody would enter arriving in London

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:56.180
in, say, 1970, 1971.

00:10:58.820 --> 00:11:02.680
Most schools of architecture

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:09.240
used to have a very clear idea of what architecture was.

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:18.050
It was directly a reflection, almost a mirror image, of what was happening in

00:11:18.050 --> 00:11:19.080
practice.

00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:25.674
Practice and the whole history of the modern movement was dictating what was

00:11:25.674 --> 00:11:27.440
happening in the school.

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:36.500
you were learning in the schools what practice had done before you. Arriving

00:11:36.500 --> 00:11:43.340
in London at that time, suddenly, or maybe not in London, maybe at the A, suddenly

00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:48.600
it was something which was completely the reverse, where the students were

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:55.580
asked to write the program, the students were asked to write the assignments. In

00:11:55.580 --> 00:11:58.100
in the diploma school for a year,

00:11:58.100 --> 00:12:02.780
they struggled trying to find what will we do?

00:12:02.780 --> 00:12:04.640
What do we propose?

00:12:05.500 --> 00:12:08.160
To me, that was absolutely fantastic

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:11.560
because that was completely reversing the logic,

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:15.300
which is still similar in a lot of places.

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:18.020
The architect provides services

00:12:18.020 --> 00:12:20.280
that are required by the client.

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:24.400
In the case of the A, that was the other way around.

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:29.400
the architect was to determine what some of the,

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:34.180
what were the needs of society.

00:12:35.060 --> 00:12:39.280
And now you realize that that was exactly in sync

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:41.460
with what Cedric was doing.

00:12:41.460 --> 00:12:45.200
But ultimately, you know, you could say,

00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:47.800
and some people might disagree,

00:12:47.800 --> 00:12:50.820
but you might say that Archigram,

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:54.020
when they were designing walking cities,

00:12:54.020 --> 00:12:58.220
or plugging cities, or instant cities,

00:12:58.220 --> 00:13:03.220
were also suggesting what society might need or want.

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:07.220
So there was a spirit of invention

00:13:07.220 --> 00:13:10.660
which was putting the responsibility

00:13:10.660 --> 00:13:13.300
back onto the architect,

00:13:13.300 --> 00:13:17.700
rather than saying, oh, we do what we're being asked.

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:19.340
And I think this is important,

00:13:19.340 --> 00:13:21.720
and that's something which is still,

00:13:21.720 --> 00:13:23.640
I do believe, valid today.

00:13:24.020 --> 00:13:29.820
If one was to ask oneself today whether this spirit is still alive,

00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:33.540
I would say yes, it's up to you entirely.

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:37.160
And there are fortunately a number of architects

00:13:37.160 --> 00:13:42.220
who will redefine the question that has been asked to them.

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:44.900
There are a number of architects who will say,

00:13:45.100 --> 00:13:48.180
hey, there is this which you need to look into,

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:50.980
which has to do with, of course, overpopulation,

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:53.280
with global warming, etc., etc.

00:13:54.020 --> 00:13:59.620
So, in other words, the questions are always there.

00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:05.620
Many architects are highly responsible.

00:14:05.620 --> 00:14:10.620
It's society itself that has perhaps forgotten to ask the questions.

00:14:10.620 --> 00:14:19.620
Today, if you want to build, you're playing a double game.

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:29.780
Let me give you an anecdote which has nothing to do but everything to do with our

00:14:29.780 --> 00:14:31.160
discussion.

00:14:34.660 --> 00:14:36.760
It's an invented anecdote.

00:14:36.760 --> 00:14:45.669
When Einstein, Albert Einstein, walks down my street in New York City 20 years or 40

00:14:45.669 --> 00:14:50.760
years from now, when there was a lot of mugging,

00:14:50.760 --> 00:14:59.495
a mugger takes his gun and points the gun at the head of Albert Einstein and asks

00:14:59.495 --> 00:15:01.760
him, what time is it?

00:15:01.760 --> 00:15:06.760
Is Einstein going to give him the theory of relativity?

00:15:06.760 --> 00:15:09.760
No, he tells him what time it is.

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:14.760
Nevertheless, what he really does is the theory of relativity.

00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:19.760
I think we find ourselves as architects always in this predicament.

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:26.760
We're talking in 2014, and the amount of recycling of Cedric,

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:31.000
of Cedric, which I see, and what the French call

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:38.000
a recuperation, using Cedric in every possible means

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:43.000
to justify things which are absolutely not worth it.

00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:48.000
I would be very, very cautious at this sort of

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:53.000
refashionable packaging of Cedric Price.

00:15:53.000 --> 00:16:04.512
I think there is a raw strength and, how can I say, I was almost going to use the

00:16:04.512 --> 00:16:06.360
word honesty,

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:15.190
in the work. And it is often, I see it used by people who use it shamelessly for

00:16:15.190 --> 00:16:16.760
purely commercial

00:16:16.760 --> 00:16:27.225
purposes? Well, generally when you teach, when you talk to students, you're trying

00:16:27.225 --> 00:16:27.480
to

00:16:27.480 --> 00:16:36.821
make them understand that architecture is a mode of thinking and therefore you use a

00:16:36.821 --> 00:16:38.600
set of examples.

00:16:38.600 --> 00:16:51.501
amusingly enough the period between 1965 and 1975, 1980, was perhaps for political

00:16:51.501 --> 00:16:52.760
reasons,

00:16:52.760 --> 00:16:58.757
certainly for political reasons, certainly for economic reasons, there was not very

00:16:58.757 --> 00:16:59.480
much work,

00:16:59.480 --> 00:17:06.291
there was the whole question that went around the 68 period, there was the

00:17:06.291 --> 00:17:07.580
disenchantment

00:17:07.580 --> 00:17:18.720
in relationship to the modernist ideals and the disenchantment in relationship to

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:19.820
what was

00:17:19.820 --> 00:17:29.959
proposed instead, in other words historicist postmodernist, hence the idea of trying

00:17:29.959 --> 00:17:30.200
to

00:17:30.200 --> 00:17:38.840
reinvent another world from other, you know, from another point of view, was

00:17:38.840 --> 00:17:46.460
important. And I'll end with my, you know, my first point, which had to do with the

00:17:46.460 --> 00:17:52.620
mode of notations. The language, the way you write the question, the way you do

00:17:52.620 --> 00:17:59.600
those diagrams, the way you do those small cartoons, it's a way to come

00:17:59.600 --> 00:18:05.900
to the heart of the ideas. It is not simply to do those great, you know, blue

00:18:05.900 --> 00:18:11.960
skies and those lovely people running among the flowers. I was one of the

00:18:11.960 --> 00:18:18.720
students representative at the ETH in Zurich. That was somewhere around 67, I

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:30.600
suspect and we were asked to suggest the name of lecturers and I was familiar, a

00:18:30.600 --> 00:18:35.280
little familiar, I read some of the magazines, Architectural Design, AD and

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:42.120
so on and I was really interested in hearing Cedric come so I asked my

00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:53.880
professors and they said no way this is not architecture so I kept a long story

00:18:53.880 --> 00:18:58.580
short I succeeded a couple years later to invite him in an

00:18:58.580 --> 00:19:05.160
independent venue called the Le Corbusier pavilion in Zurich but

00:19:05.160 --> 00:19:15.820
But meanwhile I got to know Cedric, I went to see him several times in London, and

00:19:15.820 --> 00:19:17.120
eventually

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:21.040
was interested to start to work for him.

00:19:21.040 --> 00:19:27.953
He actually offered me a job, provided that the job that he was waiting for would

00:19:27.953 --> 00:19:28.380
come.

00:19:28.380 --> 00:19:32.742
It did come, but three years later, and by that time I was teaching at the AA and I

00:19:32.742 --> 00:19:32.900
was

00:19:32.900 --> 00:19:40.880
already doing my own work, it was a little late, but that meant that a very

00:19:40.880 --> 00:19:48.680
different attitude was made available to someone like myself who had

00:19:48.680 --> 00:19:55.640
been until then being educated in a very solid manner, but in a way which

00:19:55.640 --> 00:20:02.540
was far more traditional than the role of the architect asking the questions as

00:20:02.540 --> 00:20:06.040
as opposed to the architect answering the question.
